    "Necessary in a democratic society": "The interference cannot be considered necessary in a democratic society. The freezing orders were imposed collectively, affecting the applicants and their relatives without any individualised assessment of the necessity and proportionality of the measures (see paragraphs 18 and 21). Domestic courts mechanically endorsed the freezing orders and search warrants, without addressing the lack of concrete evidence implicating specific individuals in wrongdoing (see paragraphs 21 and 27). The money-laundering investigation remained ongoing for years, prolonging the restrictions without resolution (see paragraph 17). The “foreign agent” designation was based on a minimal foreign donation of approximately EUR 1,944 and was imposed despite the FBK’s attempt to return the funds (see paragraph 30). Most critically, the extremist designation relied on broad allegations in a closed hearing with classified material, without sufficient evidence of actual extremist activity (see paragraph 35). The European Court of Human Rights has previously found that similar collective and prolonged restrictions on organisations engaged in political opposition and anti-corruption activities, lacking convincing and sufficient reasoning, are disproportionate and unnecessary in a democratic society (see Taganrog LRO and Others v. Russia, no. 32401/10; see also Kobaliya and Others v. Russia, no. 39446/16). The cumulative and severe nature of the measures, combined with their application without adequate safeguards against abuse, demonstrates that the interference exceeded what was necessary to achieve the legitimate aims pursued."
